/* Classification and Assessment, Classification, examination and scales. Original cards. */
(window.PA_CARDS = window.PA_CARDS || {}).assess = (window.PA_CARDS.assess || []).concat([
/* ---------- Classification systems ---------- */
["Classification systems","History of classification","Who first used the word 'schizophrenia', and in what year?","Eugen Bleuler, 1911","He replaced Kraepelin's term 'dementia praecox'. The name means a split between thinking, feeling and behaviour, not a split personality."],
["Classification systems","History of classification","Which psychiatrist grouped catatonia, hebephrenia and paranoid psychoses together as 'dementia praecox'?","Emil Kraepelin","He separated it from manic-depressive insanity by course: a deteriorating illness versus episodes with recovery in between."],
["Classification systems","History of classification","Who introduced the term 'schizoaffective psychosis'?","Jacob Kasanin, 1933","He described sudden psychoses with mixed schizophrenic and mood features that tended to recover, so it was first seen as a good-outcome form."],
["Classification systems","History of classification","Which subtype did Bleuler add to Kraepelin's subtypes of schizophrenia?","Simple schizophrenia","Bleuler did not think delusions or hallucinations were needed. His basic symptoms were the 'four As': associations, affect, autism and ambivalence."],
["Classification systems","History of classification","Who first described bulimia nervosa, and when?","Gerald Russell, 1979","He described it as an 'ominous variant' of anorexia nervosa, with overeating followed by vomiting or purging."],
["Classification systems","History of classification","Which 19th-century German psychiatrist argued that mental illnesses are brain diseases and backed the idea of a single 'unitary psychosis'?","Wilhelm Griesinger","He saw the different psychoses as stages of one illness process. He is often called an early biological psychiatrist."],
["Classification systems","History of classification","Which researcher is best known for the 'bipolar spectrum' idea?","Jules Angst","His long-term cohort studies found many in-between forms between unipolar and bipolar illness, so he argued for a spectrum, not a sharp divide."],
["Classification systems","Principles of classification","What do we call a system that diagnoses using clear inclusion and exclusion rules plus symptom counts and durations?","An operationalised approach","Explicit rules mean two clinicians using the same information should reach the same diagnosis, which raises reliability."],
["Classification systems","Principles of classification","Before diagnosing depression you rule out organic and psychotic causes first. Which principle is this?","The hierarchical principle","Disorders higher up (organic, then psychotic) take priority and can explain symptoms lower down (mood, anxiety)."],
["Classification systems","Principles of classification","Why are ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR called 'atheoretical'?","They define disorders by symptoms and course, not by assumed cause","The cause of most mental disorders is unknown, so a descriptive base lets clinicians of different schools agree."],
["Classification systems","Principles of classification","What is the main weakness of a categorical classification?","It forces continuous traits into yes/no boxes","Information near the cut-off is lost and comorbidity looks inflated. Dimensional ratings capture severity better."],
["Classification systems","Principles of classification","What five steps did Robins and Guze (1970) propose to validate a psychiatric diagnosis?","Clinical description, laboratory studies, delimitation from other disorders, follow-up and family studies","Each step tests whether a syndrome is distinct, stable over time and runs in families."],
["Classification systems","Principles of classification","What is the NIMH Research Domain Criteria (RDoC)?","A dimensional research framework, not a clinical manual","It studies functional domains (such as negative and positive valence systems and cognitive systems) across levels from genes to behaviour."],
["Classification systems","Multiaxial systems","How many axes did the multiaxial version of ICD-10 have?","Three","Axis 1 was the clinical diagnoses, axis 2 disability, and axis 3 contextual (psychosocial) factors."],
["Classification systems","Multiaxial systems","In DSM-IV, what was recorded on Axis V?","Global Assessment of Functioning (GAF)","Axis I was clinical disorders, II personality disorder and learning disability, III medical conditions, IV psychosocial problems."],
["Classification systems","Multiaxial systems","What did DSM-5 do with the multiaxial system?","It dropped it","Diagnoses are now listed together. GAF was removed because of poor reliability; the WHODAS 2.0 is suggested for disability."],
["Classification systems","ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR structure","When did ICD-11 come into use?","1 January 2022","The World Health Assembly adopted it in May 2019."],
["Classification systems","ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR structure","Which ICD-11 chapter holds most mental disorders, and which conditions moved out of it?","Chapter 6; sleep-wake disorders and gender incongruence moved to other chapters","Sleep-wake disorders have their own chapter. Gender incongruence moved to 'conditions related to sexual health' to reduce stigma."],
["Classification systems","ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR structure","Which new disorder did DSM-5-TR (2022) add?","Prolonged grief disorder","DSM-5-TR also added codes to record suicidal behaviour and non-suicidal self-injury."],
["Classification systems","ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR structure","Which ICD-11 grouping replaced ICD-10 'organic' categories such as organic mood disorder?","Secondary mental or behavioural syndromes associated with disorders classified elsewhere","It is used when the symptoms are judged to be a direct physical effect of a medical condition."],
["Classification systems","ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR structure","How does the style of ICD-11 diagnostic guidance differ from DSM-5-TR?","ICD-11 describes essential features with few fixed cut-offs; DSM-5-TR uses criteria sets with symptom counts","ICD-11 was built for clinical use across the world, so it leaves more room for judgement."],
["Classification systems","Cultural concepts of distress","Which DSM-5 tool helps clinicians explore how culture shapes a patient's problem?","The Cultural Formulation Interview","It has 16 questions on the patient's view of the problem, its causes, support and help-seeking."],
["Classification systems","Cultural concepts of distress","A 24-year-old man from Southeast Asia in A&E fears his penis is shrinking into his abdomen and that he will die. Name the syndrome.","Koro","It is an acute anxiety state based on a cultural belief. Small outbreaks have been described."],
["Classification systems","Cultural concepts of distress","Which culture-bound syndrome describes sudden, indiscriminate violence after a period of brooding, often followed by amnesia?","Amok","It was first described in Malaysia and mostly affects men."],
["Classification systems","Cultural concepts of distress","A 22-year-old man from South Asia believes he is losing semen in his urine and feels weak and anxious. Name the syndrome.","Dhat syndrome","Semen is seen as a vital essence in some cultures, so its supposed loss causes great worry and tiredness."],
/* ---------- Psychotic disorders ---------- */
["Psychotic disorders","Schizophrenia criteria","What are the four 'core' symptom groups for schizophrenia in ICD-11?","Persistent delusions, persistent hallucinations, disorganised thinking, and experiences of influence, passivity or control","ICD-11 needs at least two symptoms from its list, and at least one must be a core symptom."],
["Psychotic disorders","Schizophrenia criteria","What is the minimum duration of symptoms for schizophrenia in ICD-11?","One month","Most of the time during at least one month. Shorter illnesses are diagnosed elsewhere, for example acute and transient psychotic disorder."],
["Psychotic disorders","Schizophrenia criteria","What total duration does DSM-5-TR need for schizophrenia?","Six months, including at least one month of active symptoms","The rest can be prodromal or residual signs. The active phase can be shorter if treated."],
["Psychotic disorders","Schizophrenia criteria","In DSM-5-TR schizophrenia, which symptoms must be among the two required active symptoms?","At least one of delusions, hallucinations or disorganised speech","The other options are grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour and negative symptoms."],
["Psychotic disorders","Schizophrenia criteria","Are negative symptoms a 'core' symptom of schizophrenia in ICD-11?","No","They count towards the two symptoms needed but cannot be the only core feature."],
["Psychotic disorders","Schizophrenia criteria","What happened to the subtypes of schizophrenia (such as paranoid and hebephrenic) in ICD-11 and DSM-5?","They were removed","They were unstable over time and did not predict outcome or guide treatment. Symptom specifiers replaced them."],
["Psychotic disorders","Schizophrenia criteria","Name the six symptom specifiers ICD-11 uses for primary psychotic disorders.","Positive, negative, depressive, manic, psychomotor and cognitive","Each is rated for severity, which gives a dimensional picture of the current illness."],
["Psychotic disorders","Course and specifiers","A 26-year-old man diagnosed with schizophrenia 5 years ago has heard voices almost all the time since, with only short quiet spells. Which ICD-11 course specifier fits?","Continuous","Use 'continuous' when symptoms are present for nearly all the illness over at least one year, with only brief sub-threshold periods."],
["Psychotic disorders","Catatonia","Where does catatonia sit in ICD-11?","In its own grouping, at the same level as mood and psychotic disorders","It can be associated with another mental disorder, caused by a substance or medicine, or secondary to a medical condition."],
["Psychotic disorders","Catatonia","A 40-year-old man with long-standing schizophrenia on an acute ward is mute, repeats your words and holds odd postures. What is the ICD-11 diagnosis for the motor picture?","Catatonia associated with another mental disorder","Several catatonic features occur together. It is also linked with mood disorders and autism."],
["Psychotic disorders","Catatonia","Name three neurological causes of catatonia to rule out.","Encephalitis (including anti-NMDA receptor), epilepsy (including non-convulsive status) and frontal lobe lesions","Catatonia is a syndrome, not a diagnosis, so EEG, bloods and imaging are needed before blaming a psychiatric illness."],
["Psychotic disorders","Other primary psychotic disorders","How fast do symptoms reach their peak in ICD-11 acute and transient psychotic disorder?","Within two weeks, with no prodrome","Symptoms change quickly, even within a day. An episode lasts no more than 3 months, usually days to a month."],
["Psychotic disorders","Other primary psychotic disorders","How long does a DSM-5-TR brief psychotic disorder last?","At least 1 day but less than 1 month, with full return to previous function","If it lasts 1 to 6 months, the diagnosis is schizophreniform disorder."],
["Psychotic disorders","Other primary psychotic disorders","How long must delusions last for delusional disorder in ICD-11 and in DSM-5-TR?","At least 3 months in ICD-11; at least 1 month in DSM-5-TR","Both need the absence of other schizophrenia symptoms and of a mood episode to explain the delusion."],
["Psychotic disorders","Other primary psychotic disorders","A 59-year-old man has believed for 6 months that his neighbours are poisoning his water. He works normally, with no hallucinations or thought disorder. Diagnosis?","Delusional disorder","A single, well-organised delusion with normal function outside the belief and no other psychotic features is typical."],
["Psychotic disorders","Other primary psychotic disorders","Can hallucinations occur in delusional disorder?","Yes, if they are not prominent and fit the delusion","For example, feeling insects on the skin in a delusion of infestation. Clear, persistent hallucinations point to schizophrenia."],
["Psychotic disorders","Other primary psychotic disorders","What does ICD-11 need for schizoaffective disorder?","Schizophrenia and a mood episode both met at the same time in one episode, lasting at least a month","Both parts must be equally prominent. It sits in the primary psychotic disorders group."],
["Psychotic disorders","Other primary psychotic disorders","A 34-year-old man has had racing thoughts, little sleep and grandiose plans, always with equally prominent voices and delusions at the same time. In which ICD-11 grouping is his diagnosis?","Schizophrenia or other primary psychotic disorders (schizoaffective disorder)","Mood and psychotic symptoms occur together and are equally prominent, which defines schizoaffective disorder."],
["Psychotic disorders","Other primary psychotic disorders","Where is schizotypal disorder placed in ICD-11 and in DSM-5-TR?","ICD-11: primary psychotic disorders. DSM-5-TR: cluster A personality disorders (also listed in the schizophrenia spectrum)","It shares features and genetics with schizophrenia but lacks lasting delusions or hallucinations."],
["Psychotic disorders","Other primary psychotic disorders","In shared (induced) delusional disorder, which relationship is most common?","Married or cohabiting couples, then sisters","Close, isolated pairs are at risk. Separation often helps the person who adopted the belief."],
["Psychotic disorders","Outcome and associations","Give four poor prognostic factors in schizophrenia.","Male sex, early and slow onset, poor premorbid function, prominent negative symptoms","Others include cognitive impairment and long untreated illness. Mood symptoms and sudden onset are good signs."],
["Psychotic disorders","Outcome and associations","In the WHO International Pilot Study of Schizophrenia, what was the most common feature?","Lack of insight","Up to about 80% had poor insight across all cultures, so it is close to a defining feature."],
["Psychotic disorders","Outcome and associations","About what share of people with non-schizophrenic psychosis show Schneider's first-rank symptoms?","About 10% (up to about 20% in some studies)","Also, about 1 in 5 people with schizophrenia never show one, so they are not specific to schizophrenia."],
["Psychotic disorders","Outcome and associations","During acute psychosis, what most often drives depression in schizophrenia?","Fear of persecutors and hostile voices","After the acute phase, depression is linked more with shame and a sense of loss."],
["Psychotic disorders","Outcome and associations","By about how much does regular teenage cannabis use raise the risk of later schizophrenia?","About two-fold","Risk is higher with earlier use (about four-fold if used by age 15) and with high-potency cannabis."],
["Psychotic disorders","Outcome and associations","A 27-year-old man with schizophrenia stays in his room because he believes neighbours are spying on him. What kind of negative symptom is his withdrawal?","A secondary negative symptom","It comes from a positive symptom, not the illness itself. Secondary negative symptoms can improve when the cause is treated."],
["Psychotic disorders","Outcome and associations","What is typical of schizophrenia-like psychosis after traumatic brain injury?","Rare, starts 1 to 5 years after injury, linked to frontal and temporal damage","Persecutory delusions and voices are common but negative symptoms are few."],
/* ---------- Mood disorders ---------- */
["Mood disorders","Depressive episodes","What is the minimum duration for a depressive episode in ICD-11?","Two weeks","Low mood or loss of interest must be present most of the day, nearly every day, with other symptoms."],
["Mood disorders","Depressive episodes","Which symptom did ICD-11 add to the list for a depressive episode?","Hopelessness","It sits with cognitive symptoms such as guilt, poor concentration and thoughts of death."],
["Mood disorders","Depressive episodes","How does ICD-11 grade a depressive episode as mild, moderate or severe?","By intensity of symptoms and impact on function","Mild: no symptom intense, some difficulty. Moderate: marked symptoms, considerable difficulty but still working in some areas. Severe: barely able to function."],
["Mood disorders","Depressive episodes","Can a mild depressive episode in ICD-11 include psychotic symptoms?","No","Delusions or hallucinations can only be present in a moderate or severe episode."],
["Mood disorders","Depressive episodes","A 52-year-old woman stopped sleeping and working, and believes she has committed unforgivable sins. Which ICD-11 severities can include her psychotic symptoms?","Moderate or severe, with psychotic symptoms","Psychosis is allowed in both levels. Severity is graded by symptom intensity and function."],
["Mood disorders","Manic and hypomanic episodes","How long must a manic episode last in ICD-11?","At least one week, unless treatment shortens it","Elevated or irritable mood with more activity or energy, plus other symptoms, most of the day nearly every day."],
["Mood disorders","Manic and hypomanic episodes","What duration does ICD-11 give for hypomania, and what does DSM-5-TR give?","ICD-11: several days. DSM-5-TR: at least 4 days in a row","ICD-11 does not set a fixed number of days."],
["Mood disorders","Manic and hypomanic episodes","Can a hypomanic episode include psychotic symptoms?","No","Psychosis, hospital admission or severe impairment make it mania."],
["Mood disorders","Bipolar disorders","What is needed for bipolar type I?","At least one manic or mixed episode","Depressive episodes are common but not needed."],
["Mood disorders","Bipolar disorders","What is needed for bipolar type II?","At least one hypomanic and one depressive episode, never mania","One manic episode changes the diagnosis to bipolar I."],
["Mood disorders","Bipolar disorders","A 26-year-old woman has just been treated for hypomania. She had a depressive episode last year and has never been manic. What is the ICD-11 diagnosis?","Bipolar type II disorder","Hypomania plus depression, with no mania, defines bipolar II."],
["Mood disorders","Bipolar disorders","What is rapid cycling?","Four or more mood episodes in 12 months","It is linked with female sex, hypothyroidism and antidepressant use, and with poorer response to lithium."],
["Mood disorders","Bipolar disorders","In ICD-11, what is a mixed episode?","Several prominent manic and depressive symptoms at once, or changing quickly, for at least 2 weeks","DSM-5 has no mixed episode; it uses a 'with mixed features' specifier."],
["Mood disorders","Bipolar disorders","Without treatment, about how long does a manic episode last on average?","About 4 months","Untreated depression lasts about 6 months. Older people have longer episodes and shorter gaps between them."],
["Mood disorders","Persistent mood disorders","How long must mood swings last for cyclothymia?","At least 2 years","Swings are mild, and highs may feel good, so it often never reaches services."],
["Mood disorders","Persistent mood disorders","What is dysthymia?","Low mood on most days for at least 2 years, not severe enough for a depressive episode","DSM-5-TR calls it persistent depressive disorder."],
["Mood disorders","Persistent mood disorders","What is 'double depression'?","A depressive episode on top of dysthymia","Outcome is poorer and relapse more likely than with either alone."],
["Mood disorders","Perinatal and grief","What is the timing for the DSM-5-TR 'with peripartum onset' specifier?","Onset during pregnancy or within 4 weeks after delivery","ICD-11 uses a wider window of about 6 weeks after birth."],
["Mood disorders","Perinatal and grief","A 31-year-old woman 6 days after giving birth is not sleeping, wanders at night and believes the world is about to end. Which ICD-11 category fits?","Mental or behavioural disorder associated with the puerperium, with psychotic symptoms","Postpartum psychosis is an emergency. The category is not used for postpartum blues."],
["Mood disorders","Perinatal and grief","How long after a death is needed for prolonged grief disorder in ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR?","ICD-11: at least 6 months. DSM-5-TR: at least 12 months (6 months in children)","Grief must also be more intense or longer than cultural norms and impair function."],
["Mood disorders","Perinatal and grief","A 26-year-old woman whose father died 3 weeks ago avoids his road and feels he is still with her. Self-esteem is intact and she enjoys good memories. What is this?","A normal grief reaction","Sensing the dead person and waves of sadness are common. Guilt, if any, is about things left undone."],
/* ---------- Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders ---------- */
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Anxiety and fear-related","How long must symptoms last for generalised anxiety disorder in ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR?","ICD-11: at least several months. DSM-5-TR: at least 6 months","Worry or free-floating anxiety must be present more days than not, with tension and restlessness."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Anxiety and fear-related","A 25-year-old man has feared the dark since childhood but does not avoid it and is not distressed. Does he have a specific phobia?","No","A phobia needs fear out of proportion, with avoidance or great distress and impaired function."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Anxiety and fear-related","Most children with selective mutism also meet criteria for which disorder?","Social anxiety disorder","They speak normally at home but not at school. ICD-11 places selective mutism with anxiety disorders."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Anxiety and fear-related","A 40-year-old woman robbed in a tube station has since been unable to leave her flat and freezes in supermarkets, fearing she will die. Diagnosis?","Agoraphobia with panic attacks","She fears places where escape is hard. ICD-11 diagnoses agoraphobia and panic disorder separately if both are met."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Anxiety and fear-related","A 23-year-old woman who works in a warehouse avoids talking to colleagues and never goes to the pub, fearing she will embarrass herself. Diagnosis?","Social anxiety disorder","The core fear is being judged negatively by others. Blushing and trembling are common, as is misuse of alcohol."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Anxiety and fear-related","What insight specifiers does ICD-11 use for OCD and related disorders?","Fair to good insight, or poor to absent insight","Absent insight means the person is fully convinced their fears are true, but this does not make it a psychotic disorder."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Stress-associated disorders","What are the three core features of PTSD in ICD-11?","Re-experiencing in the present, avoidance, and a lasting sense of current threat","Symptoms must last at least several weeks and impair function."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Stress-associated disorders","What does complex PTSD add to PTSD in ICD-11?","Disturbances in self-organisation: poor control of emotion, a negative self-view and relationship problems","It usually follows long or repeated trauma that is hard to escape, such as childhood abuse."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Stress-associated disorders","What time windows separate DSM-5-TR acute stress disorder from PTSD?","Acute stress disorder: 3 days to 1 month. PTSD: more than 1 month","Both follow a traumatic event. Persistence beyond a month changes the diagnosis."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Stress-associated disorders","Is acute stress reaction a mental disorder in ICD-11?","No","It is listed among factors affecting health. It is a normal response that settles within days."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Stress-associated disorders","A 38-year-old woman, a teacher, saw her husband die in a car crash 3 weeks ago. She has flashbacks, nightmares and avoids driving. What is the DSM-5-TR diagnosis?","Acute stress disorder","Under a month since the trauma. If symptoms carry on past a month, PTSD is diagnosed."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Stress-associated disorders","Which PTSD feature predicts a poorer outcome?","Emotional numbing","It is linked with chronic distress, poor relationships and lower quality of life."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Stress-associated disorders","What are the time limits for adjustment disorder in ICD-11?","Starts within about 1 month of the stressor; usually settles within 6 months unless the stressor persists","It is marked by preoccupation with the stressor and failure to adapt."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Stress-associated disorders","A 21-year-old man moved from London to Dundee a month ago. He feels uneasy, cannot stop worrying about the move and cannot settle into work. Diagnosis?","Adjustment disorder","A clear stressor, onset within a month and worry about the stressor, without meeting criteria for another disorder."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Dissociative disorders","What does ICD-11 call conversion disorder, and what does DSM-5-TR call it?","ICD-11: dissociative neurological symptom disorder. DSM-5-TR: functional neurological symptom disorder","Specifiers name the symptom, such as weakness, non-epileptic seizures or speech change."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Dissociative disorders","A 30-year-old man cannot move his legs after a motorbike crash that killed his girlfriend. Tests are normal. What is the ICD-11 diagnosis?","Dissociative neurological symptom disorder, with paresis or weakness","The symptom does not fit a known nervous system disease and follows a major stressor."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Dissociative disorders","Where is Ganser syndrome placed in ICD-11?","Other specified dissociative disorders","Features include approximate answers, clouded awareness, pseudohallucinations and sudden recovery with amnesia."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Dissociative disorders","How is dissociative fugue classified in ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR?","As a form (specifier) of dissociative amnesia","There is sudden travel away from home with loss of identity, sometimes taking a new identity."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Dissociative disorders","Which memory feature suggests dissociative rather than organic amnesia?","Loss of personal identity","In organic amnesia personal identity is kept until very late. Dissociative amnesia is patchy, and skills (procedural memory) are kept."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Dissociative disorders","Is 'la belle indifférence' a reliable sign of a functional disorder?","No","Lack of concern about a disability also occurs in organic illness, such as after a stroke, so it has no diagnostic value."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Bodily distress and related","In which ICD-11 grouping is hypochondriasis placed?","Obsessive-compulsive or related disorders","It was moved from somatoform disorders. It is now also called health anxiety disorder."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Bodily distress and related","A 42-year-old man is completely sure he has a serious infection despite many normal tests, even when calm. What is the ICD-11 diagnosis?","Hypochondriasis with poor to absent insight","He holds the belief with full conviction most of the time, which the insight specifier records."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Bodily distress and related","Which ICD-11 diagnosis replaced somatisation and other somatoform disorders?","Bodily distress disorder","It focuses on distressing bodily symptoms with too much attention to them. Symptoms need not be medically unexplained."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Bodily distress and related","What replaced hypochondriasis in DSM-5?","Illness anxiety disorder (few bodily symptoms) or somatic symptom disorder (prominent bodily symptoms)","The split depends on whether bodily symptoms or health worry is the main feature."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Bodily distress and related","What separates factitious disorder from malingering?","Factitious: fakes illness to be a patient, with no clear outside reward. Malingering: fakes for an outside gain","Malingering is not a mental disorder. Both involve deliberate deception."],
["Anxiety, stress and dissociative disorders","Bodily distress and related","A 32-year-old woman, a former nurse, is found injecting faeces into her vein to cause blood infection. She gains nothing obvious. Diagnosis?","Factitious disorder imposed on self (Munchausen syndrome)","She deliberately makes herself ill to gain the sick role, not for money or other outside reward."],
/* ---------- Other ICD-11 groupings ---------- */
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Personality disorders","How does ICD-11 diagnose personality disorder?","By severity (mild, moderate, severe) with optional trait domains","The old named types were removed, except for an optional borderline pattern specifier."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Personality disorders","Name the five ICD-11 personality trait domains.","Negative affectivity, detachment, dissociality, disinhibition and anankastia","They describe the style of a person's problems, while severity describes how much they affect life."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Personality disorders","What does ICD-11 call personality problems that fall below the threshold for disorder?","Personality difficulty","It is listed among factors affecting health, not as a mental disorder."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Personality disorders","Which personality disorders make up DSM-5-TR cluster A?","Paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal","The 'odd or eccentric' cluster."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Personality disorders","Which personality disorders make up DSM-5-TR cluster B?","Antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic","The 'dramatic, emotional or erratic' cluster."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Personality disorders","Which personality disorders make up DSM-5-TR cluster C?","Avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive","The 'anxious or fearful' cluster."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Personality disorders","A 26-year-old woman who works as a secretary wants friends and a partner but keeps away from people for fear of being criticised. Which personality disorder fits?","Avoidant (anxious) personality disorder","Unlike schizoid people, who do not want close relationships, she wants them but fears rejection."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Personality disorders","A 34-year-old woman cries whenever her husband travels and cannot make daily decisions without his advice. Which personality disorder fits?","Dependent personality disorder","Features include fear of being left alone, needing others to take responsibility and difficulty disagreeing."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Personality disorders","A 20-year-old man upsets his employers by being rigid and perfectionist, cannot delegate and keeps old newspapers. Which personality disorder fits?","Obsessive-compulsive (anankastic) personality disorder","Preoccupation with rules, detail and order gets in the way of finishing tasks."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Personality disorders","Which trait is typical of paranoid personality disorder?","Excessive sensitivity to setbacks and criticism","They bear grudges and read hidden threats into harmless remarks."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Personality disorders","Which borderline personality disorder features tend to improve with age?","Impulsivity, self-harm and suicidal behaviour","Emptiness, chronic low mood and fear of abandonment tend to persist."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Neurodevelopmental disorders","By what age must several ADHD symptoms be present in DSM-5-TR?","Before age 12","DSM-IV used age 7. Symptoms must also be present in two or more settings."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Neurodevelopmental disorders","How many ADHD symptoms are needed in DSM-5-TR for children and for those aged 17 or over?","Six in a domain for children; five for age 17 and over","Symptoms must last at least 6 months and cause impairment."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Neurodevelopmental disorders","Which earlier diagnoses were merged into autism spectrum disorder in DSM-5?","Autistic disorder, Asperger's disorder, childhood disintegrative disorder and PDD-NOS","Rett syndrome was left out because it has a known genetic cause."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Neurodevelopmental disorders","When does autism spectrum disorder usually first become evident?","In the second year of life","Milder cases with normal intelligence may not be noticed until school demands rise."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Neurodevelopmental disorders","What is Rett syndrome?","An X-linked (MECP2) disorder, almost only in girls, with normal early development then loss of skills","Purposeful hand use and speech are lost, with hand-wringing, breathing problems and seizures."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Neurodevelopmental disorders","What does ICD-11 call intellectual disability, and how is severity graded?","Disorders of intellectual development; mild, moderate, severe or profound","Severity uses both measured intellect and adaptive behaviour."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Neurodevelopmental disorders","A 35-year-old man with intellectual disability works part-time as a cleaner, shops for small gifts on his own and has basic but clear speech. What level is this?","Mild","People with mild disability usually live and work with little support and have useful everyday language."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Neurodevelopmental disorders","A 42-year-old man in supported housing needs supervision for daily tasks, learns new skills slowly and has a social worker manage his money. What level of intellectual disability is likely?","Moderate","Self-care is possible with support, but independent living and managing money are not."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Neurodevelopmental disorders","A 12-year-old boy repeatedly skips school and takes drugs with friends. What is the ICD-11 diagnosis to consider?","Conduct-dissocial disorder","A repeated pattern of breaking age-appropriate rules and others' rights, such as truancy, theft or aggression."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Eating disorders","What BMI thresholds does ICD-11 use for anorexia nervosa in adults?","Significantly low: 18.5 to 14. Dangerously low: under 14","Dangerously low weight carries a high medical risk."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Eating disorders","An 18-year-old woman has a BMI of 16, exercises for hours daily and does not binge or purge. What is the full ICD-11 diagnosis?","Anorexia nervosa with significantly low body weight, restricting pattern","Restricting pattern covers weight loss through diet, fasting or exercise without binges or purging."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Eating disorders","Which two feeding and eating disorders did ICD-11 add?","Binge eating disorder and avoidant-restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID)","ARFID involves restricted eating without concern about weight or shape."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Eating disorders","A 19-year-old woman referred by her GP with possible anorexia refuses to keep mirrors at home or to know her weight. Can this show preoccupation with shape?","Yes","Preoccupation can show as repeated checking or as extreme avoidance."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Substance use and addictive behaviours","What are the three central features of alcohol dependence in ICD-11, and how many are needed?","Impaired control, increasing priority of drinking, and physical features (tolerance, withdrawal). Two of three","They are usually present over 12 months, or over 1 month if drinking is daily."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Substance use and addictive behaviours","How does DSM-5-TR grade the severity of a substance use disorder?","2 to 3 criteria mild, 4 to 5 moderate, 6 or more severe","There are 11 criteria, counted over a 12-month period."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Substance use and addictive behaviours","List the seven elements of the Edwards and Gross (1976) alcohol dependence syndrome.","Narrowed repertoire, salience, tolerance, withdrawal, relief drinking, awareness of compulsion, rapid reinstatement","Raised (not reduced) tolerance is a feature."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Substance use and addictive behaviours","Which two behavioural addictions does ICD-11 list with substance disorders?","Gambling disorder and gaming disorder","Both involve impaired control, increasing priority and continuing despite harm."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Substance use and addictive behaviours","A 49-year-old man with heavy drinking hears threatening voices while sober and fully alert, with no confusion. What is the ICD-11 term?","Alcohol-induced psychotic disorder (formerly alcoholic hallucinosis)","Consciousness is clear, unlike delirium tremens. Most cases settle quickly with abstinence."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Substance use and addictive behaviours","What is an alcoholic blackout?","A gap in memory (anterograde amnesia) for events during intoxication","Alcohol blocks the hippocampus from storing new memories, while older memories stay intact."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Sleep-wake disorders","What are the four classic features of narcolepsy?","Daytime sleepiness, cataplexy, sleep paralysis and hypnagogic or hypnopompic hallucinations","Cataplexy is sudden loss of muscle tone set off by strong emotion, such as laughter."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Sleep-wake disorders","A 21-year-old woman falls asleep mid-conversation and recently fell to the floor while laughing on a train, without losing consciousness. Diagnosis?","Narcolepsy with cataplexy","Sudden falls triggered by laughter are cataplexy, which points strongly to narcolepsy type 1."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Sleep-wake disorders","A 70-year-old man is sleepy by 7 pm and wakes very early. Which circadian disorder fits?","Advanced sleep-wake phase disorder","The body clock runs early. It is more common in older people."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Neurocognitive disorders","Which single feature best separates delirium from dementia?","Impaired, fluctuating attention and level of consciousness","These stay intact in dementia until late stages."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Neurocognitive disorders","What is the salient feature of each: frontotemporal dementia, vascular dementia and Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease?","FTD: early personality change. Vascular: stepwise decline. CJD: myoclonus with rapid decline","Alzheimer's disease classically starts with slow memory loss."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Neurocognitive disorders","About what proportion of people with amnestic mild cognitive impairment progress to Alzheimer's disease each year?","About 10 to 15%","The amnestic type is the strongest predictor of later Alzheimer's disease."],
["Other ICD-11 groupings","Neurocognitive disorders","A 23-year-old man with needle marks, weight loss and fever shows slowed thinking, poor memory and clumsiness. Which neurocognitive disorder should be considered?","HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder","It is a subcortical picture: slowing of thought and movement, apathy and poor coordination."],
/* ---------- Interview and mental state ---------- */
["Interview and mental state","Interview techniques","What is the main advantage of open questions?","They give richer information without suggesting an answer","Start with open questions and move to closed ones later."],
["Interview and mental state","Interview techniques","When are closed questions most useful?","Later in the interview to clarify details and rule out less likely symptoms","Used too early they give brief, narrow answers."],
["Interview and mental state","Interview techniques","'Do you go on holiday and are you able to relax then?' What type of question is this?","A compound question","Two questions in one confuse patients and give vague answers. Ask one at a time."],
["Interview and mental state","Interview techniques","Why should suggestive (leading) questions be avoided, especially with suggestible patients?","The answer is built into the question, so the patient just agrees","This gives false information and misleads both doctor and patient."],
["Interview and mental state","Interview techniques","'So, you have felt low for three months and stopped seeing friends. Is that right?' Which technique is this?","Summation (summarising)","It checks understanding and lets the patient correct or add details."],
["Interview and mental state","Interview techniques","A doctor says, 'Many people feel ashamed after an overdose.' Which technique is this, and what is it for?","Validation; it reduces embarrassment and supports the patient","It is supportive rather than a way to gather information."],
["Interview and mental state","Interview techniques","What is the aim of confrontation in a clinical interview?","To help the patient face something they seem to avoid or deny","It must be respectful and supportive, not used to catch the patient out."],
["Interview and mental state","Interview techniques","A doctor tells a patient, 'I am impressed by how you have kept going.' Which technique is this?","Reinforcement (affirmation)","Sincere praise builds rapport and encourages the patient to share more."],
["Interview and mental state","Interview techniques","What is good practice when using an interpreter?","Brief them first, ask for word-for-word translation, keep the same interpreter, and speak to the patient","A summarised translation can lose key signs, such as thought disorder."],
["Interview and mental state","Interview techniques","A 45-year-old Polish-speaking woman is admitted to an acute ward. Her 15-year-old son offers to interpret. What should you do?","Use a professional interpreter","Family members, especially children, may filter information and hide abuse or risk, and it puts a burden on the child."],
["Interview and mental state","Interview techniques","Why is a collateral history especially important in dementia, psychosis and personality disorder?","Patients may lack insight or not recall changes","Others can describe onset, behaviour, risk and premorbid personality."],
["Interview and mental state","Mental state examination","What is the difference between mood and affect?","Mood is the sustained inner state the patient reports; affect is the moment-to-moment emotion you observe","Mood is like the climate and affect like the weather."],
["Interview and mental state","Mental state examination","In which section of the mental state examination do you record hallucinations?","Perception","Delusions go under thought content and thought disorder under thought form."],
["Interview and mental state","Insight","What are the three parts of insight described by Anthony David?","Recognising illness, relabelling unusual experiences as illness, and accepting treatment","Insight is a range of awareness, not simply present or absent."],
["Interview and mental state","Insight","A 50-year-old man agrees he has a serious alcohol problem and takes responsibility, but has made no change and keeps drinking heavily. What level of insight is this?","Intellectual insight","He accepts the problem in words, but it has not led to change. True emotional insight leads to change in behaviour."],
["Interview and mental state","Special assessment methods","What is the diagnostic use of an amobarbital (drug-assisted) interview?","To help tell psychogenic from organic stupor or mutism","Psychogenic states often improve with the drug, while organic ones tend to get worse."],
["Interview and mental state","Special assessment methods","On a PICU, a nurse records the behaviour of a secluded 30-year-old man every 5 minutes. Which observation method is this?","Time sampling","Event sampling records chosen events instead, such as every fifth incident."],
["Interview and mental state","Special assessment methods","Which part of the history describes how the person was before they became ill?","Premorbid personality","Ask about character, interests, relationships and coping, ideally confirmed by an informant."],
/* ---------- Risk assessment ---------- */
["Risk assessment","Suicide and self-harm","What does NICE (NG225, 2022) say about using risk tools after self-harm?","Do not use tools or scales to predict suicide or to decide who gets treatment or is discharged","They predict poorly. Assess needs and risks through a full psychosocial assessment instead."],
["Risk assessment","Suicide and self-harm","Who should have a psychosocial assessment after self-harm, according to NICE?","Everyone who self-harms, by a trained mental health professional","It covers needs, risks, safeguarding and a shared safety plan."],
["Risk assessment","Suicide and self-harm","What is the strongest single predictor of suicide?","Previous self-harm","The risk is highest in the months after an episode."],
["Risk assessment","Suicide and self-harm","When is suicide risk highest after discharge from a psychiatric ward?","In the first one to two weeks","Follow-up within days of discharge is advised because of this."],
["Risk assessment","Suicide and self-harm","Does asking a patient about suicidal thoughts raise their risk?","No","Asking directly is safe and often brings relief. It is the only way to find out."],
["Risk assessment","Suicide and self-harm","Which predicts suicide more strongly: hopelessness or severity of depression?","Hopelessness","Beck's work showed hopelessness predicts eventual suicide better than depression scores."],
["Risk assessment","Suicide and self-harm","A 38-year-old man took an overdose when his wife was away, left a note and did not seek help. What do these features suggest?","High suicidal intent","Planning, precautions against being found and final acts are scored in the Beck Suicide Intent Scale."],
["Risk assessment","Suicide and self-harm","A 29-year-old woman on a mother and baby unit with postpartum psychosis believes her baby is possessed. Which risk must be assessed urgently?","Risk of harm to the baby, as well as suicide","Delusions about the infant raise the risk of infanticide."],
["Risk assessment","Violence","What is the HCR-20?","A structured professional judgement tool for violence risk","It has 20 items: 10 historical, 5 clinical and 5 risk management."],
["Risk assessment","Violence","How do actuarial and structured professional judgement methods differ?","Actuarial tools score mainly fixed factors to give a statistical estimate; structured judgement adds clinical judgement and a management plan","Structured judgement is preferred in practice because it guides how to reduce risk."],
["Risk assessment","Violence","What is the difference between static and dynamic risk factors?","Static cannot change (age, sex, past violence); dynamic can change (substance use, symptoms, adherence)","Dynamic factors are the targets of risk management."],
["Risk assessment","Violence","What is the best predictor of future violence?","Past violence","Substance misuse adds greatly to risk in people with mental illness."],
["Risk assessment","Violence","Which psychotic symptoms are most linked with violence?","Threat and control-override symptoms, and command voices the patient believes and obeys","Feeling threatened or controlled by outside forces raises the risk of acting violently."],
["Risk assessment","Violence","What is the PCL-R, and how is it scored?","Psychopathy Checklist-Revised: 20 items scored 0 to 2, maximum 40","A cut-off of 30 is used in North America; 25 is often used in UK research."],
/* ---------- Cognitive and neurological examination ---------- */
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Cognitive screening tests","What is the maximum MMSE score, and what cut-off is often used?","30; scores below about 24 suggest impairment","It takes 5 to 10 minutes. It is weak for executive problems and mild impairment."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Cognitive screening tests","What does copying intersecting pentagons in the MMSE test?","Constructional praxis (visuospatial skill)","It depends mainly on parietal function and is often lost early in dementia with Lewy bodies and Alzheimer's disease."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Cognitive screening tests","What does serial sevens test?","Attention and concentration","Subtracting 7 repeatedly from 100 also needs working memory and calculation."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Cognitive screening tests","A 74-year-old man in memory clinic scores zero on orientation to time and on delayed recall in the MMSE. How many points has he lost?","Eight","Orientation to time is worth 5 points and recall 3."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Cognitive screening tests","How is the MoCA scored, and why is it preferred for mild cognitive impairment?","Out of 30; 26 or more is normal; add 1 point for 12 years or less of education","It includes executive tasks (trail making, fluency, abstraction), so it is more sensitive than the MMSE."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Cognitive screening tests","What is the maximum ACE-III score, and which five domains does it test?","100; attention, memory, verbal fluency, language and visuospatial","Cut-offs of 88 and 82 are used. It helps separate Alzheimer's disease from frontotemporal dementia."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Cognitive screening tests","What is the Abbreviated Mental Test Score (AMTS)?","A 10-item bedside test; a score below 8 suggests impairment","It is quick and used in general hospitals to screen for delirium or dementia."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Cognitive screening tests","Which function is NOT tested by the clock-drawing test?","Orientation to time","It tests understanding, planning (executive function), visuospatial skill and constructional praxis."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Cognitive screening tests","Which bedside test assesses immediate memory?","Digit span (repeating a list of numbers)","Most adults repeat about 7 digits forward. It holds information for immediate use only."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Frontal and executive tests","How did Lezak describe executive function?","The capacity for goal-directed behaviour: volition, planning, purposeful action and effective performance","It includes starting, switching and stopping behaviour, and self-monitoring."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Frontal and executive tests","Which cognitive domain does the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test assess?","Set shifting and abstract reasoning","Patients with frontal damage keep using the old sorting rule after it changes (perseveration)."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Frontal and executive tests","What does the Stroop test measure?","Response inhibition","The patient names the ink colour of a colour word that names a different colour, so must suppress reading the word."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Frontal and executive tests","Name four bedside tests of frontal lobe function.","Verbal fluency, Luria fist-edge-palm sequence, go/no-go task, and cognitive estimates or proverbs","Trail making B tests set shifting. Line orientation and face recognition test visuospatial skills, not executive function."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Frontal and executive tests","A 45-year-old previously careful man has become rude, irritable and careless over a year. Where is the lesion likely to be?","Frontal lobe","Personality change is the classic sign of frontal damage from trauma, tumour, stroke or frontotemporal dementia."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Parietal and visuospatial tests","What are the four features of Gerstmann syndrome, and where is the lesion?","Finger agnosia, left-right confusion, acalculia and agraphia; dominant angular gyrus","It is a dominant parietal sign."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Parietal and visuospatial tests","A 67-year-old woman with eyes closed cannot name a number drawn on her palm. What is this sign?","Agraphaesthesia","It points to a lesion in the opposite parietal lobe when touch sense is otherwise normal."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Parietal and visuospatial tests","A 72-year-old man after a right hemisphere stroke denies his left arm is weak. What is this sign?","Anosognosia","Unawareness of a deficit is typical of non-dominant parietal damage, along with neglect and dressing apraxia."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Parietal and visuospatial tests","A 55-year-old man copying a complex figure keeps the fine details but loses the overall shape. Which hemisphere is likely damaged?","The right hemisphere","Right damage loses the global form (gestalt); left damage keeps the outline but loses the details."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Language","Which language functions should be tested to localise aphasia?","Fluency, comprehension, repetition, naming, reading and writing","The pattern of preserved and impaired functions points to the lesion site."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Language","How does Broca's aphasia present?","Non-fluent, effortful, telegraphic speech with good comprehension and poor repetition","The lesion is in the dominant inferior frontal gyrus. Patients are often aware and frustrated."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Language","How does Wernicke's aphasia present?","Fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension and poor repetition","The lesion is in the dominant superior temporal gyrus. Patients often lack awareness."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Language","A 61-year-old man after stroke has non-fluent speech but repeats phrases well. What type of aphasia is this?","Transcortical motor aphasia","Repetition is kept because the lesion is in front of Broca's area, sparing the repetition pathway."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Language","Which artery is involved in alexia without agraphia, and why?","Left posterior cerebral artery","Left occipital and splenium damage cuts visual input to the language area, so the patient writes but cannot read."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Agnosias and vascular syndromes","What is the triad of Balint syndrome, and where is the lesion?","Simultanagnosia, optic ataxia and oculomotor apraxia; both parieto-occipital areas","Causes include carbon monoxide poisoning and border-zone strokes."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Agnosias and vascular syndromes","Inability to recognise familiar faces with normal vision is called what?","Prosopagnosia","It is linked to damage of the fusiform gyrus, usually on both sides or on the right."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Agnosias and vascular syndromes","A 61-year-old man suddenly has right leg weakness, no speech and no movement while alert. Which artery is likely blocked?","Anterior cerebral artery","Damage to the anterior cingulate and medial frontal lobe causes akinetic mutism."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Neurological signs","How do you elicit asterixis, and what does it suggest?","Arms out, wrists bent back, eyes closed: watch for brief downward flaps; it suggests metabolic encephalopathy","It is typical of liver failure, kidney failure and carbon dioxide retention."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Neurological signs","What does pronator drift indicate?","A subtle upper motor neuron (pyramidal) lesion in the opposite hemisphere","With arms held out, palms up and eyes closed, the weak arm turns inwards and drifts down."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Neurological signs","Name four frontal release (primitive) reflexes.","Grasp, palmomental, snout and glabellar tap (sucking and rooting too)","They are normal in infants and suggest diffuse or frontal damage in adults. The cremasteric reflex is not one."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Neurological signs","How do upper and lower motor neuron lesions differ?","Upper: spasticity, brisk reflexes, upgoing plantars, little wasting. Lower: wasting, fasciculation, low tone, absent reflexes","Muscle wasting points to a lower motor neuron lesion."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Neurological signs","What does a positive Romberg test show?","Loss of joint position sense (sensory ataxia)","The patient is steady with eyes open but sways with eyes closed. It is not a test of cerebellar function."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Neurological signs","How does a resting tremor behave, and what is the usual cause?","Present at rest, reduced by movement, worse with stress; Parkinson's disease or antipsychotics","A tremor worse when reaching for a target is an intention tremor, from the cerebellum."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Neurological signs","A pupil constricts on near focus but not to light. What is it, and what is the classic cause?","Argyll Robertson pupil; neurosyphilis","Damage near the midbrain light reflex pathway spares the near reflex. Pupils are small and irregular."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Neurological signs","An 18-year-old man is picking at his clothes and confused. Extending his knee with the hip bent causes pain and resistance. What sign is this?","Kernig's sign","It suggests meningeal irritation, so meningitis or encephalitis must be excluded urgently."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Neurological signs","What does Hoover's sign help to identify?","Functional (dissociative) leg weakness","Hip extension of the weak leg returns when the patient flexes the other hip against resistance."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Neurological signs","A 24-year-old man with jaundice develops personality change, tremor and slurred speech. Which eye sign should you look for?","Kayser-Fleischer rings","Copper builds up at the edge of the cornea in Wilson's disease. A slit-lamp may be needed."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Dysarthria","What speech pattern does a cerebellar lesion cause?","Scanning (ataxic) speech","Words are broken into syllables with uneven force and rhythm."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Dysarthria","A 63-year-old man has slow, slurred, high-pitched speech and a small, stiff tongue. What is the cause?","Upper motor neuron (pseudobulbar) lesion","Spastic dysarthria comes from damage to both corticobulbar pathways, often from strokes."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Dysarthria","A 69-year-old man has slurred speech with a nasal quality and a weak, wasted tongue. What is the cause?","Lower motor neuron (bulbar) lesion","Weakness of the palate (cranial nerves IX and X) gives nasal speech; XII damage gives a flaccid tongue."],
["Cognitive and neurological examination","Dysarthria","A 45-year-old woman suddenly loses her voice after an argument, but can cough normally. Examination is normal. What is likely?","Functional (dissociative) aphonia","A normal cough shows the vocal cords work. Sudden onset after a stressor is typical."],
/* ---------- Investigations ---------- */
["Investigations","EEG","What frequency are alpha waves, and when are they seen?","8 to 13 Hz; over the back of the head when awake and relaxed with eyes closed","They disappear on eye opening or mental effort."],
["Investigations","EEG","Which EEG waves are faster than 13 Hz, and which drugs increase them?","Beta waves; benzodiazepines and barbiturates","Beta activity is mostly frontal and is common in the normal waking adult EEG."],
["Investigations","EEG","What is the usual EEG finding in delirium?","Diffuse slowing of background rhythm (more theta and delta)","It is non-specific and the slowing follows how severe the confusion is."],
["Investigations","EEG","What EEG pattern is typical of sporadic CJD?","Periodic sharp wave complexes at about 1 per second","They are not seen in variant CJD."],
["Investigations","EEG","What EEG pattern suggests hepatic encephalopathy?","Triphasic waves","They are seen in metabolic encephalopathies, especially liver failure."],
["Investigations","EEG","Why might prolactin be measured soon after a suspected seizure?","A rise within about 20 minutes supports a generalised epileptic seizure","Prolactin usually stays normal after non-epileptic attacks, but a normal level does not rule out epilepsy."],
["Investigations","Sleep studies","Which test confirms narcolepsy?","Overnight polysomnography followed by a Multiple Sleep Latency Test","Short sleep latency with two or more sleep-onset REM periods supports narcolepsy. Sleep deprivation testing is not useful."],
["Investigations","Structural imaging","How do CSF, brain, bone and fresh blood look on CT?","CSF black, brain grey, bone white, fresh blood bright white","CT shows how much X-ray each tissue absorbs."],
["Investigations","Structural imaging","Give three advantages of CT over MRI.","Better for calcification, acute bleeding and bone; faster; safe with pacemakers","MRI shows soft tissue, white matter and the back of the brain better, without radiation."],
["Investigations","Structural imaging","How do T1 and T2 MRI images differ?","T1: CSF dark, good for anatomy and contrast. T2: CSF bright, good for oedema and lesions","FLAIR is a T2 image with CSF signal removed, so lesions near the ventricles stand out."],
["Investigations","Structural imaging","Which MRI method measures white matter tract integrity?","Diffusion tensor imaging (DTI)","It tracks the direction of water movement along fibres; fractional anisotropy reflects tract integrity."],
["Investigations","Structural imaging","What is the most consistent structural brain change in schizophrenia?","Enlarged lateral and third ventricles, with reduced hippocampal volume","Changes are modest and overlap with healthy people, so imaging is not diagnostic."],
["Investigations","Structural imaging","Where is brain atrophy seen earliest in Alzheimer's disease?","Medial temporal lobe (hippocampus and entorhinal cortex)","Coronal MRI shows hippocampal atrophy; it supports, but does not prove, the diagnosis."],
["Investigations","Structural imaging","What is the pulvinar sign on MRI, and what does it suggest?","High signal in the posterior thalamus on FLAIR; variant CJD","Sporadic CJD shows cortical ribboning and high signal in the caudate and putamen on diffusion imaging."],
["Investigations","Structural imaging","A 35-year-old man with advanced HIV has new confusion. CT shows several ring-enhancing lesions. What is the likely cause?","Cerebral toxoplasmosis","It is the most common brain mass in AIDS. MRI is more sensitive than CT."],
["Investigations","Functional imaging","What signal does functional MRI measure?","The blood oxygen level dependent (BOLD) signal","Active brain areas get more oxygenated blood. The signal is indirect and gives good spatial but limited time resolution."],
["Investigations","Functional imaging","What does magnetic resonance spectroscopy measure, and what is NAA a marker of?","Brain chemicals; N-acetylaspartate marks healthy neurons","Proton MRS measures NAA, choline, creatine and glutamate. Phosphorus-31 MRS measures phosphocreatine and ATP."],
["Investigations","Functional imaging","What is the key difference between PET and SPECT?","PET can measure glucose metabolism and receptor binding; SPECT mainly measures blood flow","Both use radioactive tracers. SPECT is cheaper and more widely available."],
["Investigations","Functional imaging","What SPECT or PET pattern is typical of Alzheimer's disease?","Reduced temporal and parietal blood flow or metabolism","Frontotemporal dementia shows frontal and anterior temporal reduction; Lewy body dementia shows occipital reduction."],
["Investigations","Functional imaging","A 78-year-old woman has fluctuating confusion and vivid visual hallucinations. Which scan helps confirm Lewy body dementia?","DaT scan (dopamine transporter SPECT)","Reduced uptake in the striatum supports Lewy body dementia over Alzheimer's disease."],
["Investigations","Blood and urine tests","Which blood tests are in a standard reversible dementia screen?","Full blood count, U&Es, calcium, glucose, liver and thyroid function, B12 and folate","They look for treatable causes such as hypothyroidism and B12 deficiency."],
["Investigations","Blood and urine tests","A 32-year-old woman with abdominal pain and constipation becomes anxious and hallucinates after a barbiturate. Which test is needed?","Urine porphobilinogen (porphyrins)","Acute intermittent porphyria can be triggered by barbiturates, alcohol and oestrogens."],
["Investigations","Blood and urine tests","Which tests confirm Wilson's disease?","Low serum caeruloplasmin and high 24-hour urine copper, with slit-lamp exam for Kayser-Fleischer rings","Liver biopsy and genetic tests can confirm it."],
["Investigations","Blood and urine tests","How is Huntington's disease confirmed in someone with no known family history?","Genetic test for the CAG repeat in the huntingtin gene","40 or more repeats is fully penetrant. Pre-test counselling is essential."],
["Investigations","Blood and urine tests","A 38-year-old man with schizophrenia on a rehabilitation ward has low serum sodium and very dilute urine. What is the likely cause?","Psychogenic polydipsia","Excess water intake dilutes the blood. SIADH would give concentrated urine; diabetes insipidus gives high-normal sodium."],
["Investigations","Blood and urine tests","Which acid-base change does repeated vomiting cause in anorexia nervosa?","Hypokalaemic, hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis","Stomach acid is lost. Laxative misuse causes metabolic acidosis instead."],
["Investigations","Blood and urine tests","For how long can cannabis be found in urine?","About 3 days after occasional use; up to about 4 weeks after heavy, regular use","THC is stored in fat and released slowly."],
["Investigations","Blood and urine tests","An infusion of sodium lactate brings on palpitations and fear in a 32-year-old woman. Which disorder is she likely to have?","Panic disorder","Lactate (like carbon dioxide inhalation) provokes panic attacks in people with panic disorder far more than in controls."],
["Investigations","Blood and urine tests","A 45-year-old woman has tiredness, dizziness, abdominal pain and darker skin. Which blood results suggest Addison's disease?","Low sodium and high potassium","A short Synacthen test confirms it. Psychiatric symptoms include low mood and fatigue."],
["Investigations","Blood and urine tests","A 29-year-old man who injects heroin has a puffy face, protein in his urine and high blood pressure. What is the likely cause?","Heroin-associated nephropathy","It is a kidney disease (focal segmental glomerulosclerosis) seen in people who inject heroin."],
["Investigations","Blood and urine tests","A 40-year-old man on antipsychotics has weight gain, thirst and tiredness. Which test is most useful?","Fasting glucose or HbA1c","These symptoms suggest diabetes, a known risk of some antipsychotics."],
/* ---------- Rating scales and psychometrics ---------- */
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Depression scales","What are the key features of the Beck Depression Inventory (BDI-II)?","Self-rated, 21 items, each 0 to 3 (maximum 63), covers the past 2 weeks","It focuses on cognitive symptoms, includes a suicide item and can be used from about age 13."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Depression scales","What are the main features of the Hamilton Depression Rating Scale?","Clinician-rated, usually 17 items, with many somatic items","It is widely used in drug trials but weighs sleep, appetite and anxiety heavily."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Depression scales","Which depression scale is most sensitive to change with treatment?","Montgomery-Asberg Depression Rating Scale (MADRS)","It has 10 clinician-rated items scored 0 to 6, with few somatic items."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Depression scales","Why is the Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale useful in the medically ill?","It leaves out physical symptoms","It has 14 self-rated items, 7 for anxiety and 7 for depression, so bodily illness does not inflate the score."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Depression scales","What are the PHQ-9 range and the usual cut-off for moderate depression?","0 to 27; 10 or more","Each DSM depression symptom is rated over the past 2 weeks. GAD-7 is the matching anxiety tool."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Depression scales","Which scale screens for postnatal depression?","Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale","It has 10 self-rated items with few physical items, since sleep and energy change after birth anyway. Item 10 asks about self-harm."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Depression scales","Which depression scale is designed for older people, and why does it suit them?","Geriatric Depression Scale; simple yes/no items with few physical symptoms","Physical illness in later life would inflate scores on other scales."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Depression scales","Which scale rates depression in people with dementia?","Cornell Scale for Depression in Dementia","It combines an interview with a carer and observation of the patient."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Psychosis and mania scales","What are the features of the Young Mania Rating Scale?","Clinician-rated, 11 items; four items double-weighted (0 to 8)","The double-weighted items are irritability, speech, thought content and aggressive behaviour, so it is not self-rated."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Psychosis and mania scales","What is the structure of the PANSS?","30 items: 7 positive, 7 negative and 16 general psychopathology, each scored 1 to 7","It is a clinician-rated, semi-structured interview used to measure severity and change in schizophrenia."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Psychosis and mania scales","A 22-year-old man on an acute ward hears voices talking about him and believes he is being followed. Which clinician scale would track his symptoms over time?","PANSS (or the BPRS)","Both measure psychotic symptom severity and change. Screening tools such as the GHQ are not suitable."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Psychosis and mania scales","What are the features of the Brief Psychiatric Rating Scale?","Clinician-rated, 18 items (24 in the expanded version), each on a 7-point scale","It covers broad symptoms, is used in schizophrenia trials and needs training for good reliability."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Outcome and screening","What is the General Health Questionnaire used for?","Self-rated screening for non-psychotic 'caseness' in the community and primary care","Versions have 12, 28, 30 and 60 items. GHQ-28 scored 0 to 3 per item has a maximum of 84."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Outcome and screening","What is HoNOS?","Health of the Nation Outcome Scales: 12 clinician-rated items scored 0 to 4","Developed by the Royal College of Psychiatrists to track outcomes over time in adult services."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Outcome and screening","What does the SF-36 measure?","Health-related quality of life","It covers 8 areas, including physical function, pain, vitality, social function and mental health."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Diagnostic interviews","What is SCAN, and which older tool does it include?","Schedules for Clinical Assessment in Neuropsychiatry; it includes the PSE-10","It is a semi-structured interview by trained clinicians, giving ICD and DSM diagnoses and keeping the style of a clinical exam."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Diagnostic interviews","What is the SCID?","Structured Clinical Interview for DSM: semi-structured, clinician-administered, mainly for research","It gives reliable DSM diagnoses but needs trained interviewers."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Diagnostic interviews","Which interview can lay interviewers use to make lifetime diagnoses in large population surveys?","Diagnostic Interview Schedule (DIS)","It is fully structured, so no clinical judgement is needed. The WHO CIDI was developed from it."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Substance and eating disorder tools","What is the CAGE questionnaire, and what score is significant?","4 yes/no alcohol questions (Cut down, Annoyed, Guilty, Eye-opener); 2 or more","It is a quick screen for problem drinking, not for severity."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Substance and eating disorder tools","What are the AUDIT score range and key cut-offs?","0 to 40; 8 or more hazardous; 20 or more possible dependence","A WHO 10-item screen. AUDIT-C uses only the 3 consumption questions."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Substance and eating disorder tools","On the Short Alcohol Withdrawal Scale, what score suggests moderate to severe withdrawal needing treatment?","12 or more","Patients rate how they felt over the past 24 hours. Below 12 suggests mild withdrawal."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Substance and eating disorder tools","What is the SCOFF questionnaire used for, and what score is positive?","Screening for eating disorders; 2 or more 'yes' answers","Sick, Control, One stone, Fat, Food. It is quick for use in primary care."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Side-effect scales","Which scale rates tardive dyskinesia?","Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale (AIMS)","A 12-item clinician-rated scale of abnormal movements in face, limbs and trunk."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Side-effect scales","What does the Simpson-Angus Scale measure, and what is its main weakness?","Drug-induced parkinsonism; it is weighted towards rigidity","6 of its 10 items rate rigidity."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Side-effect scales","Which scale rates akathisia, and which is a self-rated side-effect scale?","Barnes Akathisia Rating Scale; LUNSERS (Liverpool University Neuroleptic Side-Effect Rating Scale)","LUNSERS lets patients report a wide range of antipsychotic side effects."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Intelligence and personality tests","What is the gold standard test of adult intelligence?","Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS)","It has strong norms and gives full-scale IQ and index scores. WISC is the version for children."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Intelligence and personality tests","Which test estimates premorbid IQ, and how?","The National Adult Reading Test (NART); reading irregular words aloud","Reading of familiar irregular words holds up well in early dementia, so it reflects past ability."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Intelligence and personality tests","Name three projective personality tests.","Rorschach inkblot test, Thematic Apperception Test and Draw-a-Person test","Unclear stimuli are thought to draw out hidden thoughts and feelings. Rating scales like HAM-D are not projective."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Reliability","What is test-retest reliability?","Agreement between scores from the same test given to the same person on two occasions","It assumes the person's true state has not changed in between."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Reliability","What is inter-rater reliability, and which statistic is used for categories?","Agreement between different raters scoring the same patient; Cohen's kappa","Kappa corrects for agreement by chance. Values above 0.6 show substantial agreement."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Reliability","What does internal consistency mean, and how is it measured?","How well items in a scale measure the same construct; Cronbach's alpha","Split-half reliability is another method. An alpha of about 0.7 or more is usually acceptable."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Validity","What is construct validity?","How well a test measures the theoretical concept it claims to measure","For a dementia test, it means covering the parts of cognition that dementia affects."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Validity","What is face validity?","Whether a test seems on the surface to measure what it should","It is the weakest form and is judged by simple inspection."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Validity","What is content validity?","How fully the items cover the whole domain being measured","It is often judged by experts, staff or patients reviewing the items."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Validity","A new IQ test and the WAIS are given at the same time, and the scores are correlated. Which validity is tested?","Concurrent validity","It is a form of criterion validity: comparison with a gold standard measured at the same time."],
["Rating scales and psychometrics","Validity","Higher baseline Hamilton scores predict later self-harm in untreated patients. Which validity is this?","Predictive validity","It is a form of criterion validity where the criterion is measured in the future."]
]);
